# Recipes

> **This file is a stub.** The shape exists so you know where the content belongs before there is
> any. It is marked `stub` in `.sdlc/CONFIG.md`, and that state is load-bearing: agents must not
> cite a stub. A reviewer that needs a rule from here says *"no rule exists for this"* instead of
> citing a document that says nothing.
>
> **Its first entry belongs here when:** you do the same kind of change for the **second** time.
>
> When that happens, write the entry, delete this block, and change this doc's state in
> `.sdlc/CONFIG.md` to `real`. A stub that has quietly become real is a doc nobody is allowed to
> cite and everybody assumes they can.

For each recurring change type: which files, in what order, and what is easy to miss.

The second time is the trigger on purpose. The first time you do not yet know which steps are the
non-obvious ones, and a recipe written from one instance documents that instance rather than the
change type.

## The shape of a recipe

Three sub-headings, always, because they partition the work by *how the mistake is caught*:

**A. What the compiler or the tooling will demand.** You cannot get this wrong silently.

**B. What nothing will tell you.** The registration step, the array your thing has to be added to,
the second place the same constant lives. **This is why the recipe exists.** A and C are
recoverable; B is what ships broken.

**C. What it owes a test.** Which lane, and what the test must actually assert.
